course: technology
lesson: 16
title: Electricity dangers: sockets, water, chargers and batteries
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The battery in a torch is gentle: you can hold it all day. The electricity in the wall socket is about a hundred and fifty times stronger and can stop a heart.
explain: Our bodies are mostly salty water, and salty water lets electricity flow through it, so electricity can pass through a person on its way to the ground.
explain: Electricity also makes heat: wires that carry too much, chargers buried in a duvet, or a damaged battery can all get hot enough to start a fire.
explain: Picture a bedroom at night: a phone charging under the pillow, a heater on a stack of adaptors, fairy lights left on. Each one is a small, quiet danger you can learn to spot.
explain: The rules are all «never» and «tell an adult»: never anything in a socket, never water near plugged-in things, never touch a fallen wire, and tell a grown-up about anything broken, hot or swollen.
fact: Mains electricity in UK and European homes is around 230 volts (volts measure how hard electricity pushes), enough to stop a heart, while a torch battery is only one and a half volts.
fact: Never put fingers, keys, pencils or anything else into a socket, and tell an adult if a socket is broken or scorched.
fact: Water, especially with salt or soap in it, lets electricity pass through it and through you, which is why UK bathrooms usually have no ordinary sockets, only shaver sockets.
fact: Never touch switches with wet hands or use plugged-in devices near a bath or sink, and never touch someone who is being shocked; shout for an adult to switch the power off.
fact: Each socket can only safely deliver a certain current; too many plugs on stacked adaptors, especially heaters and kettles, make wires overheat, and many house fires start this way, so high-power things like heaters are plugged straight into the wall, not into extensions.
fact: The plastic covering on a cable keeps the live wires from touching you or each other, so frayed cables and broken plugs are not used, and cables are not run under rugs.
fact: Chargers and batteries get warm; under a pillow or duvet the heat cannot escape, so phones are charged on a hard surface with a proper charger; cheap fake chargers may skip safety parts, get very hot and start fires.
fact: Lithium batteries in phones, tablets and e-scooters hold a lot of energy; if one swells, gets hot or smells, tell an adult, and never pierce or crush it.
fact: A swallowed button cell battery can burn a child's throat within hours, even if it seems flat, so a child who may have swallowed one goes to hospital at once.
fact: Power lines carry very high voltage, and electricity can jump to a kite string, especially a wet one, so kites and drones are flown in open spaces far from power lines.
fact: Birds can sit on a power line because they touch only one wire and not the ground.
fact: Never climb a pylon or into an electricity substation, even for a ball, and never touch a fallen wire; the power company gets things out safely.
fact: Substations carry a yellow warning triangle with a lightning bolt.
fact: Christmas and fairy lights are checked for damage, switched off at night, and indoor lights are never used outdoors.
exercise: Why can a torch battery not hurt you but a socket can? | The socket's voltage is far, far higher | The battery has no electricity in it | Sockets are made of metal and batteries aren't | Batteries only work in the dark
exercise: About what voltage is the mains electricity in UK homes? | Around 230 volts | Around 2 volts | Around 12 volts | Around a million volts
exercise: What do you do if a socket looks scorched or broken? | Tell an adult and don't use it | Push the plug in harder until it clicks | Poke it with a pencil to see if it works | Cover it with tape and carry on using it
exercise: Why does electricity pass through people so easily? | Our bodies are mostly salty water | Our bones are made of a kind of metal | Our skin works like a little battery | Our hair pulls electricity towards us
exercise: Why aren't there normal sockets in a UK bathroom? | Water and electricity together are dangerous | Bathrooms have no wires behind the tiles at all | Steam makes electricity too weak | Nobody needs electricity in a bathroom
exercise: Someone is being shocked by a machine. What do you do? | Don't touch them; shout for an adult to switch the power off | Pull them away by the arm | Throw a cup of water over them to stop the electricity flowing | Grab the cable and pull it out
exercise: Why can too many plugs on stacked adaptors start a fire? | Too much current makes the wires overheat | The plugs rub together and spark | Adaptors leak electricity onto the floor | The socket gets lonely and buzzes
exercise: What is the plastic covering on a cable for? | Keeping the live wires from touching you or each other | Making the cable look colourful | Making the electricity flow faster along the wire to the plug | Keeping the cable warm in winter
exercise: Why shouldn't cables go under carpets or rugs? | They get damaged by feet and can overheat unseen | Carpets suck the electricity out | The cable makes the carpet lumpy only | Rugs make the cable too cold
exercise: Why not charge your phone under the pillow? | The heat can't escape and could start a fire | The phone charges too slowly | The pillow blocks the Wi-Fi signal and the battery has to work harder | The phone might get feathers in it
exercise: What does a puffy, swollen battery mean? | It is damaged and could catch fire | It is extra full of charge and ready to go | It is cold and needs warming up on a radiator | It is brand new and still settling in
exercise: Why are button batteries so dangerous to swallow? | They can burn a child's throat within hours | They make a child glow in the dark for days | They are too big to swallow, so they never get stuck | They taste of metal, but they pass through safely
exercise: A toddler might have swallowed a button battery. What happens? | Go to hospital at once | Wait to see if they get ill | Give them a fizzy drink | Wait until the next day
exercise: Why fly kites far from power lines? | Electricity can jump to the kite string | The wind is weaker near lines | Kites scare the power company | The lines pull kites in like magnets pull pins
exercise: Why can birds sit safely on power lines? | They touch only one wire and not the ground | Their feathers are made of a kind of rubber | The lines are switched off for birds | Birds are too light to be shocked
exercise: Your ball goes over the fence of an electricity substation. What do you do? | Leave it and ask an adult to call the power company | Climb in quickly to get it | Use a long metal pole to reach it through the fence | Send a smaller child through the gap in the fence to fetch it
exercise: Why switch fairy lights off at bedtime? | Lights left on with nobody watching can overheat or start a fire | They use up all the electricity | They wake up the neighbours | They stop working in the dark
exercise: Dad plugs 2 lamps, a TV and a games console into one extension. How many plugs is that? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 2
test: my mate says it's fine to poke a socket if the switch is off. true? | No, never put anything into a socket at all | Yes, an off switch makes it totally safe, so poke away | Yes, if you use a plastic pen | No, but a metal key is fine
test: In a cartoon a kite gets stuck on a power line and a boy climbs the pylon to get it. Real rule? | Leave it and tell an adult; never climb a pylon | Climb, but wear rubber boots | Pull the string hard to free it | Throw stones at it until it falls down off the wire
test: just plug the heater into the extension with the TV and the lamps, whatever. Is that ok? | No, high-power things like heaters don't go into extensions | Yes, extensions can take anything | Yes, if the heater is small | No, but the kettle can go there instead, kettles are much safer
test: What would happen if a frayed cable's live wire touched you? | Electricity could pass through you and hurt or kill you | You would just feel a little tickle | Nothing, cables are always off | It would charge your body like a battery
test: Ruby's e-scooter battery is hot and smells funny. What should she do? | Tell an adult and keep away from it | Put it in the fridge for an hour to cool it down | Poke a hole in it to let the funny gas out | Keep riding it and see if the smell stops
test: Jack says the button battery in the remote is dead, so it can't hurt a baby. Right? | No, even a flat one can burn a baby's throat | Yes, flat batteries are harmless | Yes, they turn to sugar when empty | No, it's only brand-new ones that are dangerous to babies
test: crap, the hairdryer fell in the sink and it's still plugged in! What now? | Don't touch it; get an adult to switch the power off | Grab it out quick | Pull the cable out of the water | Pour the water out of the sink fast, then unplug it yourself
test: Which is true about birds on power lines? | They are safe because they touch only one wire and not the ground | Power lines carry no electricity at all | The birds have special rubber feet | Electricity cannot travel through animals
test: Max wants to put outdoor lights round the tree using his indoor fairy lights. Should he? | No, indoor lights are never used outdoors | Yes, they are all the same | Yes, if it doesn't rain much | No, because outdoor lights are too bright
test: A fallen wire lies across the path after a storm. What do you do? | Keep well away and tell an adult to call the power company | Step over it carefully | Move it aside with a stick | Pick it up and hang it back
test: The cheap charger from a market stall gets very hot. Why is that a worry? | Fake chargers can skip safety parts and start fires | Cheap chargers charge too fast just for fun | Hot chargers make phones waterproof | It only means it works well and fills your phone up quicker
exam: Identify the principal reason for which mains electricity presents a danger to life whereas a torch battery does not. | The mains voltage is greater by a very large factor | The battery contains no electrical energy | The mains supply consists of alternating light | The human body conducts only battery electricity
exam: It is given that a young child may have ingested a button cell battery. State the appropriate course of action. | The child is to be taken to hospital immediately | The child is to be observed for some days | The child is to be given water and put to bed | No action is required if the battery was flat or of small size
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard of an overloaded socket? | Excessive current may cause the wiring to overheat | The socket may cease to supply any electricity | The appliances may operate at reduced brightness | The socket may emit a poisonous gas
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a bird perched upon a power line. | It touches a single conductor and not the ground | Power lines are de-energised for the passage of birds | Feathers render all birds immune to electricity | Birds are too small to conduct electricity
